#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129963} :activity/id #uuid "0e238e7b-3085-5b87-b3c4-0f5c35258e51" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050820 :topic/id #uuid "6902b215-0b25-5d7a-a72f-3376ac4e0554" :topic/math-academy-id 1977} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129963 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130347 :tutorial/content "Consider the following $3\\times 3$ matrix: \n\n![](images/4f/4fd591d8028847aa7e1d11e840acdb192703d152f9c397e40fec98fd1e281c41.svg)\n\nIf we are told that one of the eigenvalues of this matrix is $\\lambda = 3,$ how do we calculate the corresponding eigenvector?\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -7 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this reduced matrix, there are two pivot columns (the $2$nd and $3$rd). Thus, $x_1$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we have $x_3 = 0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain $x_2 = 0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n![](images/70/705dd92c7330897f35671b66866791160b9d7f65e3280d5ccb9d992405c08942.svg)\n\nSetting $x_1=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n![](images/2a/2a5dd6c35fb81134f13e765e0a02be4ea2feedfd878a1923491813492739ef00.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "b1a9d148-15f7-5878-8f36-f19ee99c49d6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3566 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "875dc5aa-e4f4-50f8-a30e-d5ed46d77e32" :step/math-academy-id 20099 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129964}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129964 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129127 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128938 :question/id #uuid "d6c73b7b-4981-5908-b694-be7f0e57b612" :question/math-academy-id "e-5490"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c79885-7848-51e3-9f96-6a109e776fd5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048775}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323912} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455512}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Components of an Eigenvector Corresponding to a Given Eigenvalue"} :step/id #uuid "59537f37-7060-5544-9b4a-84501e450c50" :step/math-academy-id 20100 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129965}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129965 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129128 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128939 :question/id #uuid "b087e601-c054-563c-b7ee-43e18c74ac51" :question/math-academy-id "e-5491"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4def2d61-0093-53cf-bcbb-1d2c3e6e1738" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431011}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Eigenvector Corresponding to a Given Eigenvalue"} :step/id #uuid "8a926ac2-afd2-5b57-8911-c3b8dac86d39" :step/math-academy-id 20101 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130084}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129966 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129129 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128940 :question/id #uuid "c22ab531-3377-5827-93d2-a13854b3308c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5492"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e732a2b-a6a1-5e0f-b43e-a9fbc02cfc74" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129603} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129607} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323913} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392038}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "5d1fcdf4-d676-5ff5-bf31-d7e371f43f08" :step/math-academy-id 20102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130084 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130403 :tutorial/content "Note the following important theorem.\n\n*The set of eigenvectors $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_k \\}$ of a matrix $A,$ where each vector $\\mathbf v_i$ corresponds to a different eigenvalue, is linearly independent.*\n\n**Watch out!** Eigenvectors that correspond to the *same* eigenvalue *can* be (and very often *will* be) linearly dependent." :tutorial/id #uuid "c0f0f6d3-c9e1-52a6-bf51-244bb7751a9d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4432 :tutorial/title "Eigenvectors That Correspond to Different Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "3314b023-f15c-5f8b-a4e4-aeea702377a2" :step/math-academy-id 27265 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129966}}] :activity/title "Calculating the Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix With Distinct Eigenvalues" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129325}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477000} :activity/id #uuid "3ab8d004-5a46-5aac-836a-cb6aba195154" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050820 :topic/id #uuid "6902b215-0b25-5d7a-a72f-3376ac4e0554" :topic/math-academy-id 1977} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476985 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323914 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5988b748-db6c-5b36-9840-e6478cabbb51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321853} {:answer/id #uuid "7c472c58-699c-5782-89ce-b06e067314de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322125} {:answer/id #uuid "cf3e7fab-ed01-5f57-aecb-974f3540bbe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322766} {:answer/id #uuid "d0690c0d-0bea-5716-9be4-2e7a0d6ed043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322774} {:answer/id #uuid "dc30c442-053c-588a-a4c2-b47c4d9a660e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322869}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322766} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb178049-21d1-5f35-85d8-2469af417f05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8f655022-c86d-5349-9391-8c2ff380546e" :question/math-academy-id "q-37403" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(2)(2)) & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-2.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 0 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{5}{3} & -\\frac{20}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=4{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{3}(-2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-2(4{x}_{3})-4{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-4{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-4{x}_{3} \\\\ 4{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{-4}{1}=-4.$"} :step/id #uuid "55c470c3-9a2c-5b79-a7cb-6bb99638869c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476990}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476990 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399241 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "963f3713-419f-5a42-a8fd-36820ed2b9d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399243} {:answer/id #uuid "77d0fe15-2bfd-57f0-991d-684ad2dfd05b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399244} {:answer/id #uuid "a30c29de-8bbd-5ce5-b082-0d86e79ae9ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399245} {:answer/id #uuid "c4de3ee5-4e26-5981-b4bd-fa52650fe448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399246} {:answer/id #uuid "ed0fd701-7b05-5e6d-9f51-cb1531e040d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399247}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399247} :answer-field/id #uuid "00acfb45-f915-59a3-94c8-f02ca6e1865b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399242}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "896ad0b2-d79e-5667-ae9b-69d97f290fa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-89417" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4{x}_{3}-2{x}_{3}) \\\\  & ={x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$"} :step/id #uuid "7cbd70ad-da38-5750-9d78-fe45b60fc21e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476999}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476994 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129608 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03d27b92-6f03-55bd-b6a9-1d288bcf8c7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454911} {:answer/id #uuid "2de3eabf-cab8-5591-a492-a96a3f773d91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454970} {:answer/id #uuid "4d120a10-5675-5201-91f6-2be8ae41d759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455036} {:answer/id #uuid "5c95a6d3-17be-5589-ac03-00cd648b9d00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455069} {:answer/id #uuid "9812912f-0bf4-5fe1-b7d3-e532d4e7dbdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455178}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454911} :answer-field/id #uuid "061976f0-d2ab-56b5-ae57-17c4949d29c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b92ae563-4565-5947-89cb-f058a9c4a4a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-37398" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 9 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 13 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 9 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 13 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 9 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 10 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-12{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -14{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 9 & -7 & 0 \\\\ -14 & 10 & -8 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -14 & 10 & -8 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 9 & -7 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-7){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-(\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3})+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=3,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$"} :step/id #uuid "bd53a707-c6f5-5559-ae54-26e649e0c977" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476985}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476999 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357398 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "290f4389-144e-58ae-8aa0-f8779961574c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355007} {:answer/id #uuid "426fd857-13b1-5ab3-9758-9722e35fc330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355251} {:answer/id #uuid "435fb70d-dab8-537c-a107-ef7870828b02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355266} {:answer/id #uuid "994c1016-eda3-584d-9ca2-ccfb245592f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356025} {:answer/id #uuid "ee2df7b5-bcfb-56b5-bfec-d5975826e4f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356741}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355266} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f55dc89-7192-5755-9048-0d366538fc46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "761e0b9c-f7fe-532d-9428-c3e7936605ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-89427" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-4-\\lambda  & 0 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -3-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-3-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}-4-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-3-\\lambda )((-4-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-(-4)(-4)) & =0 \\\\ (-3-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -24) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +6)(\\lambda -4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-6.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-6.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-6.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-(-6)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+8{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=-{x}_{3}.$ From the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{3}.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{3} \\\\ -{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{2}{-1}=-2.$"} :step/id #uuid "eb3f1758-bf5d-59f0-9e9b-84fea9836cfa"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477000 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129607 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "033fe036-222d-5d10-957e-6471d19dc5f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321179} {:answer/id #uuid "2c482aed-cfdd-5bf4-acda-648b685f18fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321489} {:answer/id #uuid "2d64e163-f5c4-5413-9570-dd5493a5c5ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321500} {:answer/id #uuid "c7bd880a-2b37-5ebe-b42b-b4c304a5a933" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322697} {:answer/id #uuid "e11d49f5-3c92-5fce-b032-36bf3f972d16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322906}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322906} :answer-field/id #uuid "52e35bc9-d423-567e-8c0e-63b05f91b6ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4fce4c9f-341f-5617-8150-093fe040d196" :question/math-academy-id "q-37385" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}-2-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )((-2-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(-2)(-2)) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -6) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=0.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.$"} :step/id #uuid "f596f2be-8e67-5ffa-bdef-b19f8d8da98f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476994}}] :activity/title "Calculating the Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix With Distinct Eigenvalues" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476198}]}
